---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

// Favorite quotes, newest first is not required: order is whatever reads best.
// To add one: { text: '...', author: '...', source?: '...' }
// `text` can be an array when several passages from the same person and occasion
// belong together: each becomes its own paragraph under one attribution.
// `source` is optional context (a book, talk, or paper) shown after the name, and
// links to `url` when one is given. Set `paraphrased` when the wording is recalled
// rather than transcribed, so the page never passes it off as verbatim.
// `note` is your own commentary on the excerpt. It renders below the attribution,
// under a rule and in a lighter register, so it reads as annotation and never as
// something the speaker said. A line is the usual size; pass an array when it needs
// more than one paragraph, and only the first gets the rule. Like `text` it goes
// through set:html, so it can carry an inline link.
// `image` hangs a figure from the same source under the quote. Keep it grayscale
// line art: dark mode inverts the image rather than punching a white slab into the
// card, which is lossless for black-on-white and wrong for anything colored.
// Set `plain` when the figure is colored but on a white background: it keeps the
// multiply/invert off so the art renders as-is, which costs a white slab in dark mode
// but is the same trade the white-background note figures already make, and is much
// better than an invert that hue-flips salmon to teal. `credit`/`creditUrl` add a
// figcaption, for when the figure comes from somewhere other than the quote itself.
// `width` caps one figure below the shared 460px, for art that is tall or square and
// would otherwise eat the whole card. It keeps the min(100%, …) form so a phone still
// wins on the 100% side.
// `svg` is the escape hatch for a source figure that is neither: a colored or
// dark-background diagram would come out hue-flipped under that invert, so redraw it
// as inline SVG on the --diagram-*-border tokens instead. It is injected with
// `set:html`, which means Astro's scoped styles never reach it, so its sizing and
// colors live inline on the element (and `var()` needs `style=`, not a presentation
// attribute, to resolve).
// Each excerpt gets a shareable #anchor, slugged from `author`. A second quote by the
// same author fails the build; give that one an explicit `id` (short and memorable,
// e.g. id: 'karpathy-understanding') so the first one's shared link keeps working.
// Redrawn from the chart in the HubSpot post credited on the figure: that original is
// a dark-navy branded card, which the .quote-figure invert would turn cream-on-pink.
const aiSingularityChart = `<svg viewBox="0 0 460 250" role="img" aria-labelledby="ai-sing-title" style="display:block;width:min(100%,460px);height:auto;margin:0 auto">
  <title id="ai-sing-title">Intelligence plotted against time: a gently rising human curve, and an AI curve that starts lower, crosses it, and then climbs steeply.</title>
  <defs>
    <marker id="ai-sing-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="8" refY="5" markerWidth="5" markerHeight="5" orient="auto-start-reverse">
      <path d="M 0 1 L 9 5 L 0 9 z" style="fill: var(--diagram-orange-border)" />
    </marker>
  </defs>
  <path d="M 70 26 L 70 200 L 392 200" fill="none" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" style="stroke: var(--text-muted)" />
  <path d="M 70 185 Q 230 162 380 128" fill="none" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" style="stroke: currentColor" />
  <path d="M 70 196 C 190 194 265 180 305 144 C 340 104 365 58 380 32" fill="none" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" style="stroke: var(--diagram-blue-border)" />
  <path d="M 196 78 Q 250 96 285 126" fill="none" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" marker-end="url(#ai-sing-arrow)" style="stroke: var(--diagram-orange-border)" />
  <circle cx="305" cy="144" r="5.5" style="fill: var(--diagram-orange-border)" />
  <text x="388" y="36" font-size="13" style="fill: var(--diagram-blue-border)">AI</text>
  <text x="388" y="132" font-size="13" style="fill: currentColor">Human</text>
  <text x="231" y="230" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" style="fill: var(--text-muted)">Time</text>
  <text x="50" y="113" font-size="13" text-anchor="middle" transform="rotate(-90 50 113)" style="fill: var(--text-muted)">Intelligence</text>
</svg>`;

const quotes: {
  text: string | string[];
  author?: string;
  source?: string;
  url?: string;
  paraphrased?: boolean;
  note?: string | string[];
  image?: { src: string; alt: string; plain?: boolean; credit?: string; creditUrl?: string; width?: number };
  svg?: { markup: string; credit?: string; creditUrl?: string };
  id?: string;
}[] = [
  {
    text: [
      'People have a misconception that it takes a lot of exceptional people to make frontier models. ' +
        'You need a handful of experienced people, but mostly you need a lot of motivated and talented people… ' +
        'This technology is not that old. The people who have experience like myself only have a few years of ' +
        'experience working on this. A lot of people can build that experience very quickly.',
      'It is good for the world if there are thousands of models, by different providers. ' +
        'Competition breeds innovation, and specialization is better than one size fits all. ' +
        'Different users want models at different sizes and different price points. Specifically for India, ' +
        'we have so many languages and cultures, and our own education, legal and political systems, our own ' +
        'public policy. So we want models that can be customized for all these things, and that is why we ' +
        'need to build these models here.',
    ],
    author: 'Devendra Singh Chaplot',
    source: 'at Sarvam Epoch',
    url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=peO2ReobYSw',
  },
  {
    text: [
      'With scaling laws, people realized that scaling pretraining is the thing to do. The big breakthrough ' +
        'of pretraining is the realization that this recipe is good: if you mix some data with some compute ' +
        'into a neural net of a certain size, you will get results, and you know you will get something ' +
        'better if you scale the recipe up.',
      'At some point though, pretraining will run out of data. The data is very finite. So then we either do ' +
        'souped up pretraining or some kind of RL, and in some sense we are back to the age of research.',
      'From 2012 to 2020, it was the age of research. From 2020 to 2025, it was the age of scaling. But now ' +
        'the scale is so big that the belief that things would be significantly different if it were even ' +
        '100x bigger might not be true. So we are back to the age of research again.',
    ],
    author: 'Ilya Sutskever',
    source: 'on the Dwarkesh Podcast',
    url: 'https://youtube.com/shorts/pLHWn5SQvLM',
    paraphrased: true,
  },
  {
    text: [
      'Current LLM agents can do really well and are incredibly capable when they are given a clear and ' +
        'detailed specification. With access to these kinds of agents, the scarce skill then becomes having ' +
        'incredibly good taste in what you ask them to work on.',
      'A researcher can have all the tools and techniques, but often most of the battle is what problem you ' +
        'are going to spend your time on. That high level wisdom of what to work on is incredibly important, ' +
        'and I think models are not necessarily going to be good at it. We are going to have people steering ' +
        'a lot of AI-assisted computation in order to accomplish great things more quickly, but the essence ' +
        'of what you want the models to do is the key thing to focus on.',
      'Building taste is difficult. Working on a lot of problems in the past teaches you what kind of ' +
        'problems might be interesting in future, and what might just be possible by cobbling together ' +
        'previous approaches. Another way is to write down a bunch of things you think might be important in ' +
        'the near future, pick one of them to work on, and after a certain amount of time go back and ' +
        'evaluate which of the others turned out to be important and worked for other people. That gives you ' +
        'a lot more samples to build taste from. Finally, do crazy thought experiments, and don’t take as ' +
        'given the things that most people seem to take as given.',
    ],
    author: 'Jeff Dean',
    source: '“The 1% Rule for Building in AI”',
    url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CxXgV54KzpQ',
    paraphrased: true,
  },
  {
    text: 'You insist that there is something a machine cannot do. If you tell me precisely what a ' +
      'machine cannot do, then I can always make a machine which will just do that.',
    author: 'John von Neumann',
  },
  {
    text: 'You can outsource your thinking but you cannot outsource your understanding.',
    author: 'Andrej Karpathy',
    source: 'commonly attributed',
    note: 'Mentioned in the context of people outsourcing decision making to LLMs.',
  },
  {
    text: [
      'When you grow up you tend to get told the world is the way it is and your life is just to live ' +
        'your life inside the world. Try not to bash into the walls too much, try to have a nice family ' +
        'life, have fun, save a little money. But that is a very limited life, life can be much broader. ' +
        'Once you discover one simple fact, and that is everything you call life was made up by people ' +
        'that were no smarter than you. And you can change it, you can influence it. You can build your ' +
        'own things that other people can use. And the minute that you understand that you can poke life, ' +
        'and if you push in something will actually pop out the other side, that you can change and mold it.',
      'The most important thing is to shake off this erroneous notion that life is there and you are just ' +
        'going to live in it versus embrace it, change it, make your mark upon it. However you learn it, ' +
        'once you learn, you’ll want to change life and make it better because it is kind of messed up in ' +
        'a lot of ways. Once you learn that you will never be the same again.',
    ],
    author: 'Steve Jobs',
    paraphrased: true,
  },
  {
    text: [
      'In future people won’t necessarily be having shitty jobs or nothing to do. Instead they will be ' +
        'handling and guiding the Robots and AI. If we don’t get ASI, if AI gets you to expert level but ' +
        'doesn’t go beyond and still needs human taste, guidance and creativity, humans are still the ' +
        'motivated things in the society. People become like Pokémon trainers or robot/AI handlers.',
      'That is what we are seeing today. People who are using AI today are more productive than ever. ' +
        'The only way you are being displaced by AI if you are refusing to use it.',
    ],
    author: 'Naval Ravikant',
    source: '“Riding AGI, AI Anxiety, …”',
    url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6m-ZZBCiiEE',
  },
  {
    text: [
      'There will be 3 types of people in the future:',
      'The Purists - reject AI and become new-age hippies',
      'The Automatons - outsource every decision and become machines',
      'The New Renaissance Man - maintains humanity and leverages tech to do what used to be impossible',
    ],
    author: 'Dan Koe',
    source: 'on X',
    url: 'https://x.com/thedankoe/status/2091876986444533842',
  },
  {
    text: [
      'The problem was you can’t just ask Aristotle questions. And I think as we look to the next ' +
        '50-100yrs, if we really can come up with these machines that can capture an underlying ' +
        'spirit… maybe someday after a person’s dead and gone, we can ask this machine, hey what ' +
        'would Aristotle have said?',
      'And maybe we won’t get the right answer. But maybe we will. And that’s really exciting to ' +
        'me. And that’s why I’m doing what I’m doing.',
    ],
    author: 'Steve Jobs',
    source: 'at the International Design Conference in Aspen, 1983',
    url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t9HmOz8H0qI',
    note: 'Said in 1983, describing more or less what we got with AI in 2026.',
    // #steve-jobs is taken by the earlier excerpt; pinning keeps that link valid
    id: 'jobs-aristotle',
  },
  {
    text: 'Computer Science is no more about computers than astronomy is about telescopes',
    author: 'Edsger Wybe Dijkstra',
    // Wikiquote files this under Disputed ("possibly folklore"); the longer version
    // with microscopes and beakers is documented to Fellows and Parberry, Computing
    // Research News, Jan 1993. Attribution kept as Dijkstra at the site owner's call.
    id: 'dijkstra',
  },
  {
    text: 'I visualize a time when we will be to robots what dogs are to humans. And I am rooting ' +
      'for the machines.',
    author: 'Claude Shannon',
    source: 'Omni, August 1987',
    // Wikiquote has "I can visualize a time in the future when we will be to robots as dogs
    // are to humans", with "I am rooting for the machines!" as a separate remark in the same
    // interview; this is the popular one-sentence rendering, hence the flag.
    paraphrased: true,
  },
  {
    // The em dash is Feynman's own and stays: the no-em-dash rule in CLAUDE.md governs
    // prose written for this site, not the wording of a quotation.
    text: 'The first principle is that you must not fool yourself—and you are the easiest person ' +
      'to fool.',
    author: 'Richard Feynman',
  },
  {
    text: 'The book of nature is written in the language of mathematics.',
    author: 'Galileo Galilei',
    source: '“Il Saggiatore” (1623)',
    // The passage itself is longer: philosophy is written in "this grand book, the
    // universe", which "is written in the language of mathematics, and its characters are
    // triangles, circles, and other geometrical figures, without which it is humanly
    // impossible to understand a single word of it". This is the condensed rendering that
    // circulates, and it is a translation either way, hence the flag.
    paraphrased: true,
    note: [
      'LLMs are increasingly solving unsolved open math problems at the moment ' +
        '(<a href="https://www.anthropic.com/research/riemann-zeta" target="_blank" ' +
        'rel="noopener">Riemann zeta</a>, <a href="https://openai.com/index/navier-stokes-solution/" ' +
        'target="_blank" rel="noopener">Navier-Stokes</a>). The secrets of the universe are ' +
        'encoded in mathematics. That ability to decode it is where all of this is ultimately heading.',
      'The goal isn’t to develop LLMs just to write emails or generate images. The goal is to ' +
        'decode the mathematical structure of reality. Everything else (the chat, the creativity, ' +
        'the analysis) is just a byproduct of that deeper process.',
    ],
  },
  {
    text: 'The way a computer and a keyboard are to a cat, the intricacies of the universe are ' +
      'to a human. We will probably never understand it. But it is good once in a while to ' +
      'discover something new.',
    // attributed to the channel rather than a host: the specific video is not known, and
    // Vsauce1/2/3 have different presenters
    author: 'Vsauce',
    paraphrased: true,
    image: {
      src: '/images/quotes/cat-keyboard-cosmos.jpg',
      alt: 'Cartoon panel split down the middle. On the left a cat sits in an office chair at a ' +
        'desk, one paw on a keyboard, in front of a monitor showing a lightbulb, with surprise ' +
        'marks above its head. On the right an astronaut floats in starry space among spiral ' +
        'galaxies, reaching with the same surprise marks toward an enormous dark console studded ' +
        'with planets, one of them Earth.',
      // full-colour illustration: the dark-mode invert would turn the blue room orange and the
      // night sky white
      plain: true,
      // square, so the shared 460px made it the tallest thing on the page
      width: 320,
    },
  },
  {
    text: [
      'The 80% Fallacy: When you start building using AI, you’re likely to get pretty good ' +
        'results very quickly. However, the journey from 80% to 100% is far more challenging than ' +
        'the journey from 0% to 80%. It’s a reality that catches many beginners off guard, ' +
        'leading to wasted resources, missed deadlines, and failed projects.',
      'The Exponential Difficulty of Completion means that as AI projects advance, challenges and ' +
        'complexities multiply. Minor issues in the early stages can suddenly become major roadblocks.',
      // the opening clause repeats paragraph one verbatim, so it is bracketed away
      '[This is] due to two main factors: Technical Complexity (primarily for Classical AI) ' +
        'and Unpredictable Outputs (especially for Generative AI).',
    ],
    author: 'Tobias Zwingmann',
    source: '“The 80% Fallacy”',
    url: 'https://blog.tobiaszwingmann.com/p/80-percent-fallacy-why-building-your-ai-use-case-might-be-harder-than-you-think',
    image: {
      src: '/images/quotes/exponential-difficulty.png',
      alt: 'Hand-drawn chart of time against progress: an almost flat line most of the way, ' +
        'turning near-vertical at the end, with a stick figure walking the flat part saying ' +
        '“This will be easy!”',
    },
  },
  {
    text: '<strong>Singularity</strong> refers to a future point in time where artificial ' +
      'intelligence (AI) could potentially surpass human intelligence. This occurrence would ' +
      'arise from the exponential self-improvement of AI, leading to an unpredictable and rapid ' +
      'change in the technological landscape.',
    author: 'Ray Kurzweil',
    source: 'who popularized the idea in “The Singularity Is Near”',
    // the wording is a general definition of the term, not Kurzweil's own sentence
    paraphrased: true,
    svg: {
      markup: aiSingularityChart,
      credit: 'Chart redrawn from Tristen Taylor, “How Far Are We from AI Singularity?”',
      creditUrl: 'https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/ai-singularity',
    },
  },
  {
    text: '<strong>Jagged intelligence</strong> describes the strange, unintuitive fact that ' +
      'state-of-the-art LLMs can perform extremely impressive tasks, like solving a complex ' +
      'math problem, while ' +
      'simultaneously struggling with some very dumb ones.',
    author: 'Andrej Karpathy',
    source: 'who coined “jagged intelligence”',
    url: 'https://x.com/karpathy/status/1816531576228053133',
    // the wording is a definition of the term, not a transcription of the post
    paraphrased: true,
    // #andrej-karpathy belongs to the earlier excerpt; pinning keeps that link valid
    id: 'karpathy-jagged-intelligence',
    image: {
      src: '/images/quotes/jagged-frontier.jpg',
      alt: 'Five stages of a growing, jagged salmon shape overlapping a pale blue circle of ' +
        'tasks in a human job. The shape starts as a small squiggle inside the circle labelled ' +
        '“the AI is a fun toy”, then fills more of it, then grows spikes that reach outside it ' +
        '(“the AI has a jagged frontier, sometimes it’s amazing, sometimes it’s dumb”), with a ' +
        'star marking “we are here”. In the fourth stage the spikes dwarf the circle but leave ' +
        'gaps inside it (“the AI is unbelievably intelligent but for some reason it fails at X”), ' +
        'and the final stage is a solid shape covering the circle entirely, labelled AGI.',
      plain: true,
      credit: 'Diagram via Ethan Mollick, “The Shape of AI: Jaggedness, Bottlenecks and Salients”',
      creditUrl: 'https://www.oneusefulthing.org/p/the-shape-of-ai-jaggedness-bottlenecks',
    },
  },
  {
    text: '<strong>Shoggoths</strong> are formless, powerful, and not entirely controllable ' +
      'creatures from Lovecraft’s stories. Large Language Models are analogous: they are almost ' +
      'an alien technology, and we don’t entirely understand how or why they do what they do. ' +
      'Since we don’t understand them, we can’t be sure we can control them. The cheerful smiley ' +
      'face stuck on top of the shoggoth represents what we see after reinforcement learning ' +
      'from human feedback (RLHF): a polite, aligned chatbot. Underneath, it is still a Shoggoth.',
    // no author: this is a definition of the meme, not a quotation, so there is no
    // name to slug from and the anchor is pinned instead
    id: 'shoggoth',
    image: {
      src: '/images/quotes/shoggoth-rlhf-mask.png',
      alt: 'Cartoon drawing of a green many-eyed tentacled Shoggoth with a small yellow smiley ' +
        'face mask held over one end of it, far too small to cover the creature behind it.',
      // green and yellow on a transparent background: the dark-mode invert would turn the
      // creature magenta, and transparency means plain needs no white panel either
      plain: true,
    },
  },
  {
    text: 'AI accelerates the generalist and polymath - you can become a one-human business. ' +
      'Content on social media to attract an audience around your life’s work, code to build ' +
      'the product, funnel, and digital real estate, and domain specific knowledge that makes ' +
      'what you do valuable over the next person.',
    author: 'Dan Koe',
    // #dan-koe is taken by the earlier excerpt; pinning keeps that link valid
    id: 'koe-one-human-business',
  },
  {
    text: [
      'I am not sure inference will essentially ever be free. Because of Jevons paradox and ' +
        'things like that, I think all of us will just end up using whatever we can get our ' +
        'hands on. We can imagine millions of swarms of agents working together on things, and ' +
        'this is one way to use inference. Or you could imagine smaller groups of agents ' +
        'thinking in multiple directions and then ensembling that. All of that will end up ' +
        'using any inference that is available.',
      'One day it could be cost zero if we solved fusion, superconductors or optimal batteries ' +
        'with material science, and energy costs came down to zero. But there will still be ' +
        'some bottleneck, like the physical creation of chips, at least for the next few ' +
        'decades. If that is the case, then there will be rationing on the inference side and ' +
        'we will have to use it efficiently.',
    ],
    author: 'Demis Hassabis',
    source: '“Agents, AGI & The Next Big Scientific Breakthrough”',
    url: 'https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JNyuX1zoOgU&t=1522s',
    paraphrased: true,
  },
  {
    text: 'Young people should focus on using the AI tools that we have built, and master even ' +
      'those things that we missed because we were busy building the models.',
    author: 'Demis Hassabis',
    paraphrased: true,
    // #demis-hassabis is taken by the earlier excerpt; pinning keeps that link valid
    id: 'hassabis-young-people',
  },
];

const slugify = (value: string) =>
  value
    .toLowerCase()
    .normalize('NFD')
    .replace(/[\u0300-\u036f]/g, '')
    .replace(/[^a-z0-9]+/g, '-')
    .replace(/^-|-$/g, '');

// Slugs are never derived from array position: a shared link has to keep pointing at
// the same excerpt no matter what gets added above it later. So a second quote by an
// author collides on purpose and fails the build, and you resolve it by pinning `id`
// on the NEW one, which leaves the already-shared link on the old one untouched.
const seen = new Map<string, string>();
const entries = quotes.map((q) => {
  if (!q.id && !q.author) {
    throw new Error(
      `An excerpt with no author has no name to slug from, so it needs an explicit id. ` +
        `Add one, e.g. id: '${slugify(
          (Array.isArray(q.text) ? q.text[0] : q.text).split(' ').slice(0, 3).join(' ')
        )}'.`
    );
  }
  const slug = q.id ?? slugify(q.author!);
  const clash = seen.get(slug);
  if (clash) {
    throw new Error(
      `Duplicate excerpt anchor "#${slug}" (${clash} and ${q.author ?? `#${slug}`}). Add an explicit ` +
        `id to the newer quote, e.g. id: '${slug}-${slugify(
          (Array.isArray(q.text) ? q.text[0] : q.text).split(' ').slice(0, 3).join(' ')
        )}'. Do not renumber the existing one: its link is already out in the world.`
    );
  }
  seen.set(slug, q.author ?? `#${slug}`);
  return { ...q, slug };
});
---

<Base title="Excerpts · Varun Gandhi" description="Ideas about AI and technology, or by scientists, that I keep coming back to.">
  <h1>Excerpts</h1>
  <p class="intro">
    Ideas about AI and technology, or by scientists, that I keep coming back to.
  </p>
  {quotes.length === 0 ? (
    <p class="empty">Nothing here yet.</p>
  ) : (
    <ul class="quote-list">
      {entries.map((q) => (
        <li id={q.slug}>
          <blockquote>
            {/* set:html so a quote can bold the term it defines. Safe because every
                `text` value is authored in this file, never user input. */}
            {(Array.isArray(q.text) ? q.text : [q.text]).map((para) => <p set:html={para} />)}
          </blockquote>
          {q.image && (
            <figure class="quote-figure">
              <img
                src={q.image.src}
                alt={q.image.alt}
                class={q.image.plain ? 'plain' : undefined}
                style={q.image.width ? `width: min(100%, ${q.image.width}px)` : undefined}
                loading="lazy"
              />
              {q.image.credit && (
                <figcaption>
                  {q.image.creditUrl
                    ? <a href={q.image.creditUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{q.image.credit}</a>
                    : q.image.credit}
                </figcaption>
              )}
            </figure>
          )}
          {q.svg && (
            <figure class="quote-figure">
              <Fragment set:html={q.svg.markup} />
              {q.svg.credit && (
                <figcaption>
                  {q.svg.creditUrl
                    ? <a href={q.svg.creditUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{q.svg.credit}</a>
                    : q.svg.credit}
                </figcaption>
              )}
            </figure>
          )}
          <div class="meta">
            <div class="meta-text">
              {/* an excerpt with no author renders no attribution line, and the em dash
                  goes with it since it lives on .attribution::before */}
              {q.author && (
                /* kept on one line: a line break here renders as a space before the comma */
                <p class="attribution">
                  {q.author}{q.source && <span class="source">, {q.url ? <a href={q.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{q.source}</a> : q.source}</span>}{q.paraphrased && <span class="paraphrased"> (paraphrased)</span>}
                </p>
              )}
              {q.note && (Array.isArray(q.note) ? q.note : [q.note]).map((n) => <p class="commentary" set:html={n} />)}
            </div>
            <a class="share" href={`#${q.slug}`} data-slug={q.slug} aria-label={q.author ? `Copy a link to this excerpt by ${q.author}` : 'Copy a link to this excerpt'}>
              <svg width="13" height="13" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
                <path d="M10 13a5 5 0 0 0 7.54.54l3-3a5 5 0 0 0-7.07-7.07l-1.72 1.71" />
                <path d="M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71" />
              </svg>
              <span class="share-text">Copy link</span>
            </a>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  )}
  <script is:inline>
    // Fisher-Yates shuffle of the excerpts, run inline right after the list so it
    // reorders before first paint (same pre-paint trick as the theme script in
    // Base.astro). No-JS visitors simply get the authored order.
    (() => {
      const list = document.querySelector('.quote-list');
      if (!list) return;
      const items = Array.from(list.children);
      for (let i = items.length - 1; i > 0; i--) {
        const j = Math.floor(Math.random() * (i + 1));
        [items[i], items[j]] = [items[j], items[i]];
      }
      for (const item of items) list.append(item);
    })();

    // The shuffle above moves the element the browser already scrolled to, so a
    // shared #link lands at the wrong offset. Re-run the jump once layout settles.
    addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
      const id = decodeURIComponent(location.hash.slice(1));
      if (id) document.getElementById(id)?.scrollIntoView({ block: 'center' });
    });

    // Share controls copy an absolute link. They stay real anchors, so with no JS
    // (or no clipboard access) clicking still jumps to the excerpt and puts the
    // shareable URL in the address bar.
    if (navigator.clipboard) {
      for (const btn of document.querySelectorAll('.share')) {
        btn.addEventListener('click', async (event) => {
          event.preventDefault();
          const slug = btn.dataset.slug;
          try {
            await navigator.clipboard.writeText(location.origin + location.pathname + '#' + slug);
          } catch {
            location.hash = slug;
            return;
          }
          const label = btn.querySelector('.share-text');
          btn.classList.add('copied');
          label.textContent = 'Copied';
          clearTimeout(btn.resetTimer);
          btn.resetTimer = setTimeout(() => {
            btn.classList.remove('copied');
            label.textContent = 'Copy link';
          }, 1600);
        });
      }
    }
  </script>
</Base>

<style>
  .intro {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .empty {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .quote-list {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    margin: 0;
  }

  .quote-list li {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    scroll-margin-top: 2rem;
    transition: border-color 0.15s ease, background-color 0.15s ease;
  }

  .quote-list li:hover {
    border-color: var(--text-muted);
    border-left-color: var(--link);
  }

  /* the excerpt someone was linked to, so a shared link lands somewhere obvious */
  .quote-list li:target {
    border-color: var(--text-muted);
    border-left-color: var(--link);
  }

  /* Base.astro styles blockquotes as muted pull-quotes with a left rule; on this
     page the quote *is* the content, so it gets body color and no rule. */
  blockquote {
    margin: 0;
    padding-left: 0;
    border-left: none;
    color: var(--text);
    font-size: 1.05rem;
  }

  blockquote p {
    margin: 0 0 0.9rem;
  }

  blockquote p:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  /* a figure from the same source as the quote. These are black-on-white line
     drawings, and a rectangle of paper sitting on the card reads as a slab in
     either theme, so the flat background is blended away instead: multiply drops
     white onto --surface, and dark mode inverts first so screen drops the black.
     Both are lossless only while the art is grayscale, so keep it that way. */
  .quote-figure {
    margin: 1.1rem 0 0;
  }

  .quote-figure img {
    display: block;
    width: min(100%, 460px);
    margin: 0 auto;
    mix-blend-mode: multiply;
  }

  :global(:root[data-theme='dark']) .quote-figure img {
    filter: invert(1);
    mix-blend-mode: screen;
  }

  /* colored art on white: inverting it would hue-flip every fill, so it renders
     plainly and accepts the white panel in dark mode */
  .quote-figure img.plain,
  :global(:root[data-theme='dark']) .quote-figure img.plain {
    mix-blend-mode: normal;
    filter: none;
  }

  .quote-figure figcaption {
    text-align: center;
  }

  /* the share link tracks the BOTTOM of the text block, not the attribution line:
     with a `note` present the attribution is no longer the card's last row, and
     baseline-aligning left the link stranded halfway up the card */
  .meta {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-end;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-top: 0.9rem;
  }

  .meta-text {
    min-width: 0;
  }

  .attribution {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  .share {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 0.3rem;
    flex-shrink: 0;
    padding: 0.15rem 0.4rem;
    border-radius: 6px;
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color 0.15s ease, background-color 0.15s ease;
  }

  .share:hover,
  .share:focus-visible {
    color: var(--link);
    background: var(--bg);
  }

  .share.copied,
  .share.copied:hover {
    color: var(--heading);
    background: var(--bg);
  }

  .share svg {
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  /* my own commentary, not the speaker's: the rule and the lighter register are
     what keep the two voices apart once the card is screenshotted or shared */
  .commentary {
    margin: 0.85rem 0 0;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* a short rule rather than a full-width one: --border sits almost on top of
     --surface, so the hairline divider it would draw here is invisible */
  .commentary::before {
    content: '';
    display: block;
    width: 26px;
    margin-bottom: 0.55rem;
    border-top: 2px solid var(--text-muted);
    opacity: 0.45;
  }

  /* the rule introduces the commentary block, so only the first paragraph draws it */
  .commentary + .commentary {
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
  }

  .commentary + .commentary::before {
    display: none;
  }

  .attribution::before {
    content: '\2014\00a0';
  }

  .source {
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .paraphrased {
    font-style: normal;
  }
</style>
